there's been a lot of talk recently  about digital currencies and everyone's  got their own ideas of how we should do  it i personally have been on television  multiple times saying the united states  should and will digitize the us dollar  now we have representative lynch coming  out and putting forward the electronic  currency and secure hardware act also  known as the ecash act the idea is to  create digital dollars but use many of  the same aspects of physical cash in a  digital form if we go ahead and we take  a look at what this legislation calls  for what we can see is that first it  directs the secretary of the treasury to  develop and introduce a form of retail  digital dollar called e-cash the whole  idea is that it's peer-to-peer privacy  respecting has no transaction fees and  it's payable to bearer just like  physical cash it also moves forward and  asked to establish an electronic  currency innovation program within the  us treasury to test and evaluate  different forms of secure hardware-based  e-cash  and then as you would expect it goes on  to ask to establish an independent  monetary privacy board to oversee and  monitor the federal government's efforts  to preserve monetary privacy this is  really fascinating because then you get  a special purpose ring fence treasury  overdraft account at the federal reserve  bank of new york ultimately what this  legislation is trying to accomplish is  it's trying to take all the ideas of  physical cash and digitize them but of  course the first thing that people ask  is why is legislation being put forward  to create these digital dollars isn't  this just what a central bank digital  currency does  well not necessarily in the eyes of the  legislation the ecas act it would define  ecash as being legal tender a bearer  instrument interoperable replicating  anonymity and privacy respecting  features of physical cash and it would  promote equity and justice but it's not  necessarily a central bank digital  currency they explicitly call out and  say ecash is not a central bank digital  currency and it is not in competition  with a central bank digital currency  either now this opens up an entire  pandora's box if we go ahead and we take  a look at all the various forms of  digital cash what we can see is that  there's a lot of them there's  cryptocurrencies there's stable coins  there's bank deposit central bank  reserves cbdc's fed accounts or  treasuries and e-cash and what you can  see is that ultimately every single  person who's interested in this idea  has their own way of doing things the  market is going to have to sift through  all these ideas we're going to have to  come to a conclusion as to what the  consensus for digital dollars will be  e-cash is one of many ideas central bank  digital currency is another idea that's  being put forward ultimately i don't  know whether it's going to matter or not  because what we have seen is that the  current versions of digital dollars and  stable coins whether we are talking  about tether usdc or ust about 200  billion dollars in the market the  market's doing just fine using them as  digital dollars hey you did you like  this video great we make five of them a  day and post them here on this channel  make sure you subscribe like the video  and see you next time
 

